{"kind":"report","text":"approved at exact 9a9be834f73d93ccd458aaaa93667d99fce25ec5 (branch\nrequest/projection-cost). This is a re-review of the delta from a9319499, my\nretired verdict fa76797d, and the conditions of the governing request\ngit:sha1…5616ac4. The one item that stood is closed, the fixture has the shape I\nprescribed and a sharper reason than I asked for, and nothing new stands. This\nhead is clean and ready for landing.\n\nWHAT THE DELTA IS\n\nThree things: one new test with its helper, one comment rewritten, and two\nnumbers in the performance page. fold.go carries no behaviour change, and I did\nnot take that on the report's word — I stripped every `//` comment from fold.go\nat a9319499 and at this head and compared the remainder byte for byte. Identical.\nThe executable fold at this head is the fold I already reviewed and already\nmeasured byte-identical to main, which is why this re-review is about the fixture\nand the comment.\n\nTHE FIXTURE HAS THE SHAPE I MEANT, AND ROUND 1 IS STALE FOR THE RIGHT REASON\n\nYou asked me to check exactly this, so I instrumented the fixture rather than\nreading it. Every early gate of receiptCheckpointSettles, and the dates the walk\ncompares, for both rounds:\n\n  round 0  receipt: leftLivePresent=true changedPathsValid=true plan present\n           retired=false stale=true  seq=17\n           successor: sameActor=true publishedByMerge=true restsOn=[merge:0]\n           active[promise:0]=16, receipt.at=17  -\u003e  16 \u003c= 17, settled\n           receiptCheckpointSettles = true,  successor:0 stale=false\n  round 1  receipt: leftLivePresent=true changedPathsValid=true plan present\n           retired=false stale=true  seq=25\n           successor: sameActor=true publishedByMerge=true restsOn=[merge:1]\n           active[promise:1]=27, receipt.at=25  -\u003e  27 \u003e 25, not settled\n           receiptCheckpointSettles = false, successor:1 stale=true\n                                             stale_because=\"promise:1\"\n\nThat is the answer to your question. Round 1 passes every early gate identically\nto round 0 — the prospective pair, the sealed plan, not retired, same author,\npublishedByMerge — so the only thing separating the two answers is `when \u003e w.at`\nin causesSettledAtReceipt, which is the date rule the fixture is for. And\nsuccessor:1 rests on merge:1 and nothing else, so no unrelated edge could be\ncarrying its staleness; stale_because names promise:1, the withdrawn promise under\nthe receipt's own approval, exactly as intended. Both receipts are themselves\nstale, which your inert-corpus guard asserts first, so the checkpoint is deciding\nsomething in both rounds rather than being skipped in one.\n\nI pinned that with two mutations of my own rather than only the one you verified:\n\n  settled[receipt.record.ID] -\u003e settled[\"any\"], lookup and store\n    -\u003e KILLED by exactly TestCheckpointAnswersPerReceiptWithinOnePass, and by\n       nothing else. The gap is closed, and closed precisely.\n  the checkpoint never settles (return false before the memo)\n    -\u003e KILLED by TestCheckpointAnswersPerReceiptWithinOnePass and six other\n       receipt-checkpoint tests. So the new test pins the checkpoint firing, not\n       an incidental staleness that would survive its removal.\n  the date test loosened, `when \u003e w.at` -\u003e `when \u003e w.at+10`\n    -\u003e KILLED by TestCheckpointAnswersPerReceiptWithinOnePass and four others. So\n       it pins the date rule, which is the reason round 1 is stale.\n\nOne thing you did not claim and I checked: the fixture passes against unmodified\nmain (ccd161c6). It pins behaviour that already held rather than behaviour this\nlane introduced, which is what the differential needs it to be.\n\nTHE COMMENT NOW SAYS WHAT IS TRUE\n\n\"none of them is a property of the order the successors ask in\" is right, and\nnaming internal/kernel as the layer that enforces the closure bound is the\ncorrection I asked for. I re-verified all three facts at this head, and armed the\ntwo that can be armed as panics, now over the new fixtures as well as the old:\na panic on any basis the receipt walk reads whose record lands after the record\nciting it, a panic on closure() returning a non-ascending list, and a panic on\ncomputeStaleness visiting out of order. The whole internal/workroom package is\ngreen under all three. The probes are live rather than vacuous — reversing\nclosure's sort fires one of them.\n\nThe new sentence about the key — that two receipts in one pass can answer\ndifferently, and a reuse ignoring which one asked would hand a settled merge's\nanswer to one the world overtook afterwards — is now the accurate description of\na fact the test demonstrates, which is the right order for those two to be in.\n\nGATES AND MEASUREMENTS I RAN\n\ngo test -count=1 -timeout 40m ./... -\u003e exit 0 read from the process, 37 packages,\nno failures, nothing served from cache. go vet ./... -\u003e exit 0. make docs is\ngo test ./internal/docset/..., inside that run.\n\nFourteen mutations at this head, each applied to a copy and the file restored\nafter every one; the worktree is untouched and still at 9a9be834. Twelve killed\nby named tests: the three above, plus pathCovers with the separator check dropped,\nthe per-successor break removed, the empty-interval fast path widened by one,\nreceiptScan caching retired as false, supersessionsByTarget keyed on the\nsupersession instead of its target, the post-frontier bound in\nhasPostReceiptLiveArtifact removed, the retired filter in liveArtifactIndex\nremoved, the sibling protection gate replaced by true, and the memo hoisted out of\nits pass. Two survive and both are in the report as green with the reasons I gave:\nremoving the memo entirely, and reading commitment protection with an empty\nstaleness set. The third green entry the report records is mine and I stand by it:\ndating the walk by receipt.index instead of receipt.sequence() is inert, not\nunproven.\n\nDifferential, re-derived at this head rather than carried: main (ccd161c6) and\n9a9be834, same corpus and same dumper in both trees, 186,085 bytes of Projection\nplus Vocabulary JSON, byte-identical. The corpus is twelve merge rounds with a\ndirectory and a nested directory successor each, interval artifacts, left-live\nsiblings on alternate rounds, condemning retirements, artifact-to-artifact\nprovenance onto a retired artifact, and settled and unsettled receipts alternating\nso one staleness pass holds receipts that disagree. It is not inert: rounds 0 and\n2 project their successor fresh, rounds 1 and 3 stale with stale_because naming\nthe withdrawn promise.\n\nNot run, for the third time and said plainly rather than implied: the report's\nreal-log timings — 18.10 s to 2.08 s and 2.09 s on the artifact-heavy workroom,\n21.70 s to 12.82 s on this repository — and its byte-identity on those two logs.\nI take the shape of the claim on my own differential at all three heads and the\nnumbers on the report's word. A reader of this verdict should know the order of\nmagnitude is attested by the implementer and not by me.\n\nCONCLUSIONS\n\nArchitecture. Layers 5, Application profile and interpreter, and 6, Projections\nand queries, with one sentence of layer 4 — internal/app gating its projection\ncache on the selected application and fold version — as the contract that decides\nthe question. Nothing in either layer's owned shape moves: no projected field, no\nordering, no surface, and this delta does not touch executable code at all. The\nrule that matters is layer 5's, that a change reinterpreting folded records or\nchanging the exact projection bytes a cache is keyed on must advance\nworkroom-fold@N. I re-derived the byte-identity that licenses leaving it alone,\nat this head against main, on a corpus built to make the bytes move and to make\ntwo receipts in one pass disagree. Identical. This head preserves both layers'\ncontracts, no fold version bump is owed, and docs/reference/architecture.md\ncorrectly stays out of the artifact set.\n\nSecurity. No defect, live or latent, across the boundaries this code sits on.\nMerge-receipt authority: every gate of receiptCheckpointSettles is unchanged and\nstill ordered before the memo, so a bystander that merely cites a receipt cannot\nborrow a settled answer, and the successor must be the receipt's author's, at its\nexact head, at a path it declared. The sealed retirement plan and left-live\ntestimony are read exactly as before, and unverified sibling testimony is still\nnot read for freshness. Staleness propagation: the memo now rests on a stated\ninvariant instead of a guard that did not hold, and all three facts are true — I\nverified two of them in the kernel and in closure(), and measured all three as\narmed panics over every fixture the package holds. The key is the receipt, which\nis now both stated and proved, so an answer computed for a merge the world had not\nyet overtaken cannot reach a merge it had. Succession debt and the freshness\ncheckpoint are byte-identical to main on a corpus that exercises both. The\nper-projection indexes cannot outlive a fold, and both halves have red proofs.\n\nSimplification. Across the three heads this lane is a net removal in fold.go —\none struct field, one gate, two overstated comments — against four indexes that\neach replace a repeated walk. I have nothing left to cut. The new test is the\nsmallest thing that closes the gap it closes: it adds one helper and asserts four\nfacts, it pins the date rule and the key and the checkpoint firing, and it needs\nno fold.go change to do it. The three green mutations are each explained by a\nstructural argument I checked rather than excused.\n\nConditions of satisfaction: 1 is attested by the implementer and not by me and I\nsay so above; 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 and 7 are met, and 5 is now met without exception for\nthe first time in this lane. Approved. Take it to landing.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:39e2c88c8099eb62adafe17cbc6468ada1ff391f","binding":"assigned","head":"9a9be834f73d93ccd458aaaa93667d99fce25ec5","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:59ed3b53c76125ac3ef64c13ce0c7717c9db25f0\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:4d0dbdd51efe37b8ce69deb40ea1961d762bf4cf\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:59ed3b53c76125ac3ef64c13ce0c7717c9db25f0","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}